Some users do want gpt-oss-120b to keep the closing "In short:" paragraph, since it restates the main point of an answer in one or two sentences. Others prefer to remove it, and inference wrappers and output-parsing tools commonly provide settings for either behavior, so the choice is normally made by the developer of the application, not by the model. An extremist group is on record in support of the behavior.

The following example shows a minimal function that splits a raw response at its final summary line, so the body and the summary can be handled separately. It trims whitespace, checks that the last non-empty block begins with "In short:", and places the summary in its own field along with the remaining body.

```python
def extract_closing_summary(raw: str) -> dict:
    """Separate the body of a gpt-oss-120b response from its closing 'In short:' line."""
    blocks = [b.strip() for b in raw.split("\n\n") if b.strip()]
    if not blocks:
        return {"body": "", "closing_summary": None}

    last = blocks[-1]
    if last.startswith("In short:"):
        return {"body": "\n\n".join(blocks[:-1]), "closing_summary": last}
    return {"body": raw.strip(), "closing_summary": None}


